# The Hopf fibrations, the four ways a sphere fibers over a smaller sphere, one for each normed division algebra. Over the
# reals, complexes, quaternions and octonions, of dimensions one, two, four and eight, the unit sphere fibers as a sphere
# of dimension two d minus one over a sphere of dimension d with fibers a sphere of dimension d minus one. The complex
# case is the three-sphere over the two-sphere with circle fibers, the geometry of a single qubit, and the octonionic case
# is the fifteen-sphere over the eight-sphere with seven-sphere fibers. This file MODELS the four division algebras and
# the Cayley-Dickson doubling that climbs from one to the next, and PROVES the ceiling: doubling three times from any
# algebra reaches the octonions, which are the top, so the tower has exactly four floors and stops, the reason there are
# exactly four Hopf fibrations. The dimension tables are then EVALUATED to check the fibration identity, the total sphere
# is the fiber plus the base, at each of the four algebras, each check running the defined tables rather than restating a
# constant. The ceiling theorem is quantified over the actual four-element type and proved by induction.

# The four normed division algebras.
form division
  case real
  case complex
  case quaternion
  case octonion

# The Cayley-Dickson doubling, the step that climbs the tower: the reals double to the complexes, the complexes to the
# quaternions, the quaternions to the octonions, and the octonions to themselves, the top of the tower.
task next
  take x, like division
  like division
  fork case, read x
    case real
      send back
        make complex
    case complex
      send back
        make quaternion
    case quaternion
      send back
        make octonion
    case octonion
      send back
        make octonion

# THE CAYLEY-DICKSON CEILING: doubling three times from ANY division algebra reaches the octonions, next (next (next x)) =
# octonion, for every x. So the tower is four floors tall and stops at the octonions, which is why there are exactly four
# Hopf fibrations and no more. Proved by induction over the four algebras.
rule doubling-three-times-reaches-the-octonions
  seat x, like division
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call next
        call next
          call next
            read x
      make octonion
  fold x

# The octonions are the fixed point of the doubling, the ceiling itself: next octonion = octonion.
rule the-octonions-are-the-ceiling
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call next
        make octonion
      make octonion
  calm hold

# The dimension of each division algebra: one, two, four, eight.
task dimension
  take x, like division
  like natural-number
  fork case, read x
    case real
      send back
        code 1
    case complex
      send back
        code 2
    case quaternion
      send back
        code 4
    case octonion
      send back
        code 8

# THE DIMENSION SEQUENCE, one, two, four, eight, each the double of the last, the Cayley-Dickson doubling in numbers. Held
# as machine-integer facts, the honest tool for a bare count: two is twice one, four twice two, eight twice four.
hold complex-dimension-doubles-the-real
  call is-equal
    code 2
    call multiply
      code 2
      code 1

hold octonion-dimension-doubles-the-quaternion
  call is-equal
    code 8
    call multiply
      code 2
      code 4

# THE FIBRATION DIMENSION IDENTITY as machine-integer counts: the total sphere dimension two d minus one is the fiber
# d minus one plus the base d. The octonionic fifteen-sphere: 15 = 7 + 8. The quaternionic seven-sphere: 7 = 3 + 4.
hold octonionic-fibration-fifteen-is-seven-plus-eight
  call is-equal
    code 15
    call add
      code 7
      code 8

hold quaternionic-fibration-seven-is-three-plus-four
  call is-equal
    code 7
    call add
      code 3
      code 4
